course: dictionary
lesson: 1179
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. A dictionary lists them in order, and you choose the meaning that fits the sentence. «A broody hen, one that wants to sit on eggs, keeps her eggs warm for about three weeks.» The words about eggs and warmth tell you that broody means wanting to sit on eggs.
explain: Now try a story sentence: «After the bad news, Mum sat by the window, quiet and broody.» There are no hens here, so look at the mood around the word. Quiet, moody and gloomy thoughts fit the sentence. Always ask: does my meaning still make sense when I put it in?
explain: Grown-ups sometimes use broody in a different way. In the UK, «feeling broody» means wanting to have a baby. You will mostly hear this in grown-up talk, so the sentence and the speaker's situation will show you which meaning is meant.
fact: Broody has three meanings, so always check the sentence to choose the right one.
fact: «Broody» (of a hen) means wanting to sit on her eggs to keep them warm until they hatch.
fact: «Broody» (informal, UK) means wanting to have a baby.
fact: «Broody» also means quiet, moody and thinking gloomy thoughts.
fact: To hatch means to come out of an egg. Before hatching, the chicks peep inside the shell, and the hen clucks back.
exercise: «A broody hen keeps her eggs warm for about three weeks.» What does broody mean here? | *Wanting to sit on her eggs until they hatch | Wanting to have a baby | Very hungry | Quiet and gloomy
exercise: «Before hatching, the chicks peep inside the shell.» What does hatching mean? | Being sold at the market | *Coming out of the egg | Falling off the nest | Getting lost in the farmyard
exercise: «After the bad news, Sam sat by the window, quiet and broody.» What does broody mean here? | Full of energy and running about | Wanting to have a baby | Wanting to sit on eggs | *Quiet, moody and thinking gloomy thoughts
test: Ella says, «Mum has been feeling broody lately.» In grown-up UK talk, what could broody mean? | Lost in the woods | Covered in feathers | Very hot and sticky | *Wanting to have a baby
test: «The broody hen would not leave the nest, even for food.» What does broody mean here? | Wanting a baby | *Wanting to sit on her eggs | Quiet and sad | Shining in the dark
exam: Which sentence uses broody to mean quiet and moody? | «The broody farmer counted his eggs.» | «The hen was broody on her nest all week.» | «The chicks were broody and peeped at the sky.» | *«Tom was broody all evening, staring at the rain.»
